        "heading": "Kossel-Lewis approach & Octet Rule",
        "content": "\n      <p>The study of <strong>Kossel-Lewis approach & Octet Rule</strong> covers the structural orientations, electronic configurations, and chemical characteristics of elements. In JEE examinations, this domain demands rigorous conceptual clarity of electronic screening, orbital overlap, and coordination principles. We represent the molecular parameters or crystal fields in these complexes as:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$\\text{Bond Order} = \\frac{N_b - N_a}{2}$$</div>\n      <p>Molecular Orbital Theory formula representing bond stability and length.</p>\n      \n      <p>The chemical reaction modeling this atomic system under standard laboratory parameters is represented as:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$\\text{Bond Order} = \\frac{N_b - N_a}{2}$$</div>\n    \n      <p>Key topics include electronic configuration anomalies, trends in shielding constants, hydration enthalpies, and color characteristics resulting from $d-d$ transitions or charge transfers. Students should pay close attention to diagonal relationships and anomalous trends in electronegativity. Memorizing periodic trends without understanding effective nuclear charge variations is a recipe for errors in Advanced multiple-concept tests.</p>\n    ",
