          {
            "question": "Calculate the solubility product constant ($K_{sp}$) of Silver Chloride ($AgCl$) if its solubility in water is $1.3 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ mol/L}$ at 298 K.",
            "solution": "1. Write the dissolution reaction:$$AgCl(s) \\rightleftharpoons Ag^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq)$$2. Set up the solubility equilibrium: if solubility is $s$, then $[Ag^+] = s$ and $[Cl^-] = s$.\\n3. Apply the $K_{sp}$ formula:$$K_{sp} = [Ag^+][Cl^-] = s^2$$4. Substitute values:$$K_{sp} = (1.3 \\times 10^{-5})^2 = 1.69 \\times 10^{-10}$$The solubility product is $1.69 \\times 10^{-10}$.",
            "tip": "Precipitation occurs when the ionic concentration product exceeds the solubility product ($Q > K_{sp}$).",
            "difficulty": 2
          }
