        "content": "\n      <p>Organic transformations of <strong>Pyrolysis & Cracking of Alkanes</strong> are dictated by electronic displacements, reaction intermediates, and thermodynamic stability parameters. The mechanistic pathways of substitution, addition, and elimination are analyzed using kinetic rates and stereocontrol. We model the thermodynamic and free-energy barrier equations for these reactions as:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$C_n H_{2n+2} + \\left(\\frac{3n+1}{2}\\right)O_2 \\longrightarrow n CO_2 + (n+1) H_2O$$</div>\n      <p>Balanced combustion equation for alkanes under excess oxygen.</p>\n      \n      <p><strong>Reaction Mechanism & Synthesis:</strong> Reduces aromatic rings to 1,4-cyclohexadienes using Sodium or Lithium metal in liquid Ammonia in the presence of alcohol:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$C_6H_6 + 2Na + 2ROH \\stackrel{\\text{liq. } NH_3}{\\longrightarrow} \\text{1,4-cyclohexadiene} + 2NaOR$$</div>\n      <p>This mechanism highlights the electron push. Notice the nucleophilic attack vector and the leaving group dissociation. We can analyze intermediate stability (e.g., resonance of carbocations or transition states) to predict major and minor products under thermodynamic or kinetic control.</p>\n    \n      <p>To master these pathways, you must write out detailed reaction mechanisms step-by-step, tracing the electrophilic and nucleophilic sites. Important factors include resonance structures, inductive effects, hyperconjugation, and steric hindrance. For instance, when evaluating substitution rates, the nature of the solvent, the leaving group ability, and carbocation stability controls the product outcome entirely.</p>\n    ",
