        "syllabusNote": "Syllabus weightage checks show this area covers 7.8% of direct competitive questions.",
        "solvedExamples": [
          {
            "question": "Calculate potential of Zn-Cu cell ($E^\\circ = 1.10\\text{ V}$) when $[Zn^{2+}] = 0.1\\text{ M}$ and $[Cu^{2+}] = 0.01\\text{ M}$.",
            "solution": "1. Cell reaction: $Zn + Cu^{2+} \\rightleftharpoons Zn^{2+} + Cu$, so $n=2$ and $Q = \\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Cu^{2+}]} = \\frac{0.1}{0.01} = 10$.\\n2. Apply Nernst: $E = 1.10 - \\frac{0.0591}{2}\\log(10) = 1.10 - 0.0295(1) = 1.07\\text{ V}$.",
            "tip": "A higher reactant concentration increases cell potential, whereas higher product concentration decreases it.",
            "difficulty": 2
          }
        ]
