        "heading": "Thermodynamics of Metallurgy: Ellingham Diagram analysis",
        "content": "\n      <p>The study of <strong>Thermodynamics of Metallurgy: Ellingham Diagram analysis</strong> covers the structural orientations, electronic configurations, and chemical characteristics of elements. In JEE examinations, this domain demands rigorous conceptual clarity of electronic screening, orbital overlap, and coordination principles. We represent the molecular parameters or crystal fields in these complexes as:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{spontaneous reduction}$$</div>\n      <p>Metal oxide with more negative ΔG can reduce another metal oxide</p>\n      \n      <p>The chemical reaction modeling this atomic system under standard laboratory parameters is represented as:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{spontaneous reduction}$$</div>\n    \n      <p>Key topics include electronic configuration anomalies, trends in shielding constants, hydration enthalpies, and color characteristics resulting from $d-d$ transitions or charge transfers. Students should pay close attention to diagonal relationships and anomalous trends in electronegativity. Memorizing periodic trends without understanding effective nuclear charge variations is a recipe for errors in Advanced multiple-concept tests.</p>\n    ",
