        "heading": "Classification and Biological Importance of Carbohydrates",
        "content": "\n      <p>Organic transformations of <strong>Classification and Biological Importance of Carbohydrates</strong> are dictated by electronic displacements, reaction intermediates, and thermodynamic stability parameters. The mechanistic pathways of substitution, addition, and elimination are analyzed using kinetic rates and stereocontrol. We model the thermodynamic and free-energy barrier equations for these reactions as:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$pI = \\frac{pK_{a1} + pK_{a2}}{2}$$</div>\n      <p>Isoelectric point calculation of neutral amino acids, where net molecular charge is zero (zwitterion form).</p>\n      \n      <p>The typical chemical reaction path representing this functional group conversion is modeled as:</p>\n      <div class='math-block'>$$pI = \\frac{pK_{a1} + pK_{a2}}{2}$$</div>\n    \n      <p>To master these pathways, you must write out detailed reaction mechanisms step-by-step, tracing the electrophilic and nucleophilic sites. Important factors include resonance structures, inductive effects, hyperconjugation, and steric hindrance. For instance, when evaluating substitution rates, the nature of the solvent, the leaving group ability, and carbocation stability controls the product outcome entirely.</p>\n    ",
